<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-chasles-relation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Chasles' relation
  </title>
  
  <statement>
    <p>
      Let <m> f \colon X \to \R </m> be a real-valued function defined on a closed interval <m> X \subsetneq \R </m>. Prove that if <m> f </m> is Riemann integrable, then
      
      <md>
        \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
      </md>
      
      for all real numbers <m> a , b , c \in X </m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
  
  <hint>
    <p>
      <alert> You do not need to integrate any functions for this problem! </alert> Just address the possible orderings of <m> a </m>, <m> b </m>, and <m> c </m> and apply <xref ref="lemma-chasles-relation"/>.
    </p>
  </hint>
  
  <!-- <solution>
    <p>
      We note that <xref ref="lemma-chasles-relation"/> implies that the restrictions of <m> f </m> to the closed intervals between <m> a </m>, <m> b </m>, and <m> c </m> are Riemann integrable.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      We first address the cases in which <m> a </m>, <m> b </m>, and <m> c </m> are not all distinct. If <m> a = b </m>, then <m> \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = 0 </m>, and so
      
      <md>
        \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = 0 + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
      </md>.
      
      Similarly, if <m> b = c </m>, then <m> \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = 0 </m>, and so
      
      <md>
        \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + 0 = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
      </md>.
      
      Finally, if <m> a = c </m>, then <m> \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = 0 </m>, and so
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } \amp = 0 = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } - \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ a }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
    
    <p>
      We can now address the cases in which <m> a </m>, <m> b </m>, and <m> c </m> are all distinct. If <m> a \lt b \lt c </m>, then <xref ref="lemma-chasles-relation" text="title"/> implies that
      
      <md>
        \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
      </md>.
      
      If <m> a \lt c \lt b </m>, then <xref ref="lemma-chasles-relation" text="title"/> implies that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } \amp = \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ c }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } - \int_{ c }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      If <m> b \lt a \lt c </m>, then <xref ref="lemma-chasles-relation" text="title"/> implies that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } \amp = -\int_{ b }^{ a }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ a }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      If <m> b \lt c \lt a </m>, then <xref ref="lemma-chasles-relation" text="title"/> implies that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } \amp = -\int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } - \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = -\int_{ b }^{ a }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      If <m> c \lt a \lt b </m>, then <xref ref="lemma-chasles-relation" text="title"/> implies that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } \amp = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } - \int_{ c }^{ a }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } - \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = -\int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } - \int_{ c }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
      
      Finally, if <m> c \lt b \lt a </m>, then <xref ref="lemma-chasles-relation" text="title"/> implies that
      
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \int_{ a }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } \amp = -\int_{ c }^{ a }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = -\int_{ c }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } - \int_{ b }^{ a }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
        
        <mrow>
          \amp = \int_{ a }^{ b }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } } + \int_{ b }^{ c }{ f \of{ x } \, \diff{ x } }
        </mrow>
      </md>.
    </p>
  </solution> -->
</exercise>
